{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Y46RTBZ3njNBp76LxtaFjq","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tname: submit_training_job CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES failure\n3\tdescription: submit_training_job does not correctly pass CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES to the training process, causing GPU conflicts and OOM\n4\ttype: feedback\n5\toriginSessionId: 232e5244-d448-419b-9dd3-45fbb335ed4d\n6\t---\n7\tNever use `submit_training_job` when specific GPU assignment is required. It fails to propagate `CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES` to the actual training process, causing the job to land on cuda:0 regardless of what GPU was specified.\n8\t\n9\t**Why:** On 2026-05-15, inject10-r2 (PID 95247) and inject25 (PID 95170) both OOM'd on cuda:0 because `submit_training_job` didn't pass `CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES` correctly, conflicting with the seed123 training already on cuda:0. The original inject10 (PID 94635) worked because it was submitted via manual tmux with `export CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=2` before the swift command.\n10\t\n11\t**How to apply:** Use `ssh_execute` + manual tmux submission with explicit `export CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=<N>` before the training command. Format: `tmux new-session -d -s <name> \"bash -c 'source conda && export CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=<N> && swift sft ...'\"`. Verify with `nvidia-smi` after 10-15 seconds.\n12\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cac5a696-6b6e-48ad-ab92-08181aa0595a","uuid":"81baeead-6c93-4f7e-8e3d-f3c3f212ebb2","timestamp":"2026-05-15T02:55:22.315Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.claude-account-2/projects/-home-ubuntu--agent-ml-research-idea-gen-0514-2-projects-tax-error-inject-agent/memory/feedback_cuda_visible_devices.md","content":"---\nname: submit_training_job CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES failure\ndescription: submit_training_job does not correctly pass CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES to the training process, causing GPU conflicts and OOM\ntype: feedback\noriginSessionId: 232e5244-d448-419b-9dd3-45fbb335ed4d\n---\nNever use `submit_training_job` when specific GPU assignment is required. It fails to propagate `CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES` to the actual training process, causing the job to land on cuda:0 regardless of what GPU was specified.\n\n**Why:** On 2026-05-15, inject10-r2 (PID 95247) and inject25 (PID 95170) both OOM'd on cuda:0 because `submit_training_job` didn't pass `CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES` correctly, conflicting with the seed123 training already on cuda:0. The original inject10 (PID 94635) worked because it was submitted via manual tmux with `export CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=2` before the swift command.\n\n**How to apply:** Use `ssh_execute` + manual tmux submission with explicit `export CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=<N>` before the training command. Format: `tmux new-session -d -s <name> \"bash -c 'source conda && export CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=<N> && swift sft ...'\"`. Verify with `nvidia-smi` after 10-15 seconds.\n","numLines":12,"startLine":1,"totalLines":12}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017nWcCDsWTcfJBo6NJKxRxv","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Insights from Negative Results in NLP EMNLP 2026 reviewer guidelines review form\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Call for Main Conference Papers - EMNLP 2026\",\"url\":\"https://2026.emnlp.org/calls/main_conference_papers/\"},{\"title\":\"LINGUIST List 37.972 Confs: 2026 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing (Hungary)\",\"url\":\"https://linguistlist.org/issues/37/972/\"},{\"title\":\"CFP | Insights 2024\",\"url\":\"https://insights-workshop.github.io/2021/cfp/\"},{\"title\":\"ARR Reviewer Guidelines\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines\"},{\"title\":\"Insights 2024 | Workshop on Insights from Negative Results in NLP\",\"url\":\"https://insights-workshop.github.io/\"},{\"title\":\"EMNLP: Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing 2027 2026 2025 ...\",\"url\":\"http://www.wikicfp.com/cfp/program?id=883\"},{\"title\":\"Advice on Reviewing for EMNLP\",\"url\":\"https://2020.emnlp.org/blog/2020-05-17-write-good-reviews/\"},{\"title\":\"EMNLP: Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing\",\"url\":\"https://dl.acm.org/doi/proceedings/10.5555/1613715\"},{\"title\":\"2026 Reviewer Guidelines\",\"url\":\"https://neurips.cc/Conferences/2026/ReviewerGuidelines\"},{\"title\":\"Call For Papers: The Second Workshop on Insights from Negative Results (EMNLP 2021) | ACL Member Portal\",\"url\":\"https://www.aclweb.org/portal/content/call-papers-second-workshop-insights-negative-results-emnlp-2021-0\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, I found information about EMNLP 2026's approach to negative results and reviewer guidelines:\n\n## EMNLP 2026 and Negative Results\n\nEMNLP 2026 has a goal of curating a diverse technical program—in addition to traditional research results, papers may contribute negative findings, survey an area, announce the creation of a new resource, argue a position, report novel linguistic insights derived using existing computational techniques, and reproduce (or fail to reproduce) previous results.\n\n## Reviewer Guidelines\n\nEMNLP 2026 will use ACL Rolling Review (ARR) as a reviewing system, but final decisions will be made by the conference. Both submissions of articles for review and commitment of reviewed articles to the conference will be performed via the Open Review platform.\n\nThe search results also reference the ARR Reviewer Guidelines, which provide general guidance for reviewers. ARR developed detailed guidelines and suggestions for each step of the process, including a list of common problems in both reviews and NLP papers to watch out for.\n\n## Background on the \"Insights from Negative Results\" Workshop\n\nThe results also contain information about the separate \"Workshop on Insights from Negative Results in NLP,\" which is an ongoing workshop series. Publication of negative results is difficult in most fields, but in NLP the problem is exacerbated by the near-universal focus on improvements in benchmarks. This situation implicitly discourages hypothesis-driven research, and it turns creation and fine-tuning of NLP models into art rather than science.\n\nFor specific EMNLP 2026 reviewer guidelines and review forms, you may want to check the official EMNLP 2026 website or the ACL Rolling Review platform directly.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cac5a696-6b6e-48ad-ab92-08181aa0595a","uuid":"d56e2b7d-b8fa-4324-a122-60fcecfe40a5","timestamp":"2026-05-15T02:55:25.709Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Insights from Negative Results in NLP EMNLP 2026 reviewer guidelines review form","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_017TfhKSnHUawbUVXXkB24QV","content":[{"title":"Call for Main Conference Papers - EMNLP 2026","url":"https://2026.emnlp.org/calls/main_conference_papers/"},{"title":"LINGUIST List 37.972 Confs: 2026 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing (Hungary)","url":"https://linguistlist.org/issues/37/972/"},{"title":"CFP | Insights 2024","url":"https://insights-workshop.github.io/2021/cfp/"},{"title":"ARR Reviewer Guidelines","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines"},{"title":"Insights 2024 | Workshop on Insights from Negative Results in NLP","url":"https://insights-workshop.github.io/"},{"title":"EMNLP: Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing 2027 2026 2025 ...","url":"http://www.wikicfp.com/cfp/program?id=883"},{"title":"Advice on Reviewing for EMNLP","url":"https://2020.emnlp.org/blog/2020-05-17-write-good-reviews/"},{"title":"EMNLP: Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing","url":"https://dl.acm.org/doi/proceedings/10.5555/1613715"},{"title":"2026 Reviewer Guidelines","url":"https://neurips.cc/Conferences/2026/ReviewerGuidelines"},{"title":"Call For Papers: The Second Workshop on Insights from Negative Results (EMNLP 2021) | ACL Member Portal","url":"https://www.aclweb.org/portal/content/call-papers-second-workshop-insights-negative-results-emnlp-2021-0"}]},"Based on the web search results, I found information about EMNLP 2026's approach to negative results and reviewer guidelines:\n\n## EMNLP 2026 and Negative Results\n\nEMNLP 2026 has a goal of curating a diverse technical program—in addition to traditional research results, papers may contribute negative findings, survey an area, announce the creation of a new resource, argue a position, report novel linguistic insights derived using existing computational techniques, and reproduce (or fail to reproduce) previous results.\n\n## Reviewer Guidelines\n\nEMNLP 2026 will use ACL Rolling Review (ARR) as a reviewing system, but final decisions will be made by the conference. Both submissions of articles for review and commitment of reviewed articles to the conference will be performed via the Open Review platform.\n\nThe search results also reference the ARR Reviewer Guidelines, which provide general guidance for reviewers. ARR developed detailed guidelines and suggestions for each step of the process, including a list of common problems in both reviews and NLP papers to watch out for.\n\n## Background on the \"Insights from Negative Results\" Workshop\n\nThe results also contain information about the separate \"Workshop on Insights from Negative Results in NLP,\" which is an ongoing workshop series. Publication of negative results is difficult in most fields, but in NLP the problem is exacerbated by the near-universal focus on improvements in benchmarks. This situation implicitly discourages hypothesis-driven research, and it turns creation and fine-tuning of NLP models into art rather than science.\n\nFor specific EMNLP 2026 reviewer guidelines and review forms, you may want to check the official EMNLP 2026 website or the ACL Rolling Review platform directly."],"durationSeconds":6.521232587999999}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AQnM6v5TiuCynisoLJ7Arw","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"Insights from Negative Results in NLP\" workshop EMNLP 2025 2024 reviewer guidelines call for papers\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Call for Main Conference Papers - EMNLP 2026\",\"url\":\"https://2026.emnlp.org/calls/main_conference_papers/\"},{\"title\":\"CFP | Insights 2024\",\"url\":\"https://insights-workshop.github.io/2021/cfp/\"},{\"title\":\"Workshop on Insights from Negative Results | ACL Member Portal\",\"url\":\"https://www.aclweb.org/portal/content/workshop-insights-negative-results\"},{\"title\":\"Advice on Reviewing for EMNLP\",\"url\":\"https://2020.emnlp.org/blog/2020-05-17-write-good-reviews/\"},{\"title\":\"Insights 2024 | Workshop on Insights from Negative Results in NLP\",\"url\":\"https://insights-workshop.github.io/\"},{\"title\":\"Workshop on Insights from Negative Results in NLP | ACL Member Portal\",\"url\":\"https://www.aclweb.org/portal/content/workshop-insights-negative-results-nlp\"},{\"title\":\"Call For Papers: The Second Workshop on Insights from Negative Results (EMNLP 2021) | ACL Member Portal\",\"url\":\"https://www.aclweb.org/portal/content/call-papers-second-workshop-insights-negative-results-emnlp-2021-0\"},{\"title\":\"Connectionists: Final Call For Papers: Workshop on Insights from Negative Results (EMNLP 2020)\",\"url\":\"https://mailman.srv.cs.cmu.edu/pipermail/connectionists/2020-July/034667.html\"},{\"title\":\"The Third Workshop on Insights from Negative Results in NLP | ACL Member Portal\",\"url\":\"https://www.aclweb.org/portal/content/third-workshop-insights-negative-results-nlp-0\"},{\"title\":\"Instructions for Reviewers for EMNLP 2020\",\"url\":\"https://2020.emnlp.org/blog/2020-05-26-instructions-for-reviewers/\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, I found relevant information about the \"Insights from Negative Results in NLP\" workshop. Here's what I found:\n\n## Workshop Information\n\nThe Workshop on Insights from Negative Results in NLP will be held in Budapest, Hungary, October 22-29, 2026 (co-located with EMNLP, with the exact day TBA).\n\n## Workshop Purpose\n\nThe workshop addresses the problem that publication of negative results is exacerbated in NLP by the near-universal focus on improvements in benchmarks. This situation implicitly discourages hypothesis-driven research, and it turns creation and fine-tuning of NLP models into art rather than science, while increasing the time, effort, and carbon emissions spent on developing and tuning models. The workshop invites both practical and theoretical unexpected or negative results that have important implications for future research, highlight methodological issues with existing approaches, and/or point out pervasive misunderstandings or bad practices.\n\n## Submission Guidelines\n\nThe workshop accepts short papers (up to 4 pages, excluding references), as well as 1-2 page non-archival abstract submissions for papers published elsewhere.\n\n## Reviewer Guidelines and Ethics\n\nPer conference guidelines, a paper that may raise ethical issues needs to explicitly discuss them, and that discussion will be taken into account in the review process. If the paper has been rejected from another venue, the authors will have the option to provide the original reviews and the author response. The new reviewers will not have access to this information, but the organizers will be able to take into account the fact that the paper has already been revised and improved.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cac5a696-6b6e-48ad-ab92-08181aa0595a","uuid":"df396cf5-0927-4603-bf59-b33023d999b6","timestamp":"2026-05-15T02:55:28.108Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"\"Insights from Negative Results in NLP\" workshop EMNLP 2025 2024 reviewer guidelines call for papers","results